\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024} on LIBERO-Goal~\cite{liu2023_libero} (10 tasks, 50 demonstrations each).
Representations are extracted from six sources spanning the VLA pipeline (\S\ref{sec:repr_extraction}): DinoSigLIP+Projector, LLM image\_mean and readout tokens at each of 32 layers, DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024_dinov2} (\texttt{cls\_token}/\texttt{patch\_mean}), CLIP~\cite{radford2021_clip} (\texttt{patch\_mean}), and branch-split (DINOv2 1{,}024-D / SigLIP 1{,}152-D before fusion).
Three LLM conditions are compared: VLA-trained, pretrained Llama-2 base (no VLA fine-tuning), and untrained (6.74B parameters reinitialized to random weights), crossed with instruction presence (\S\ref{sec:model_data}).
RSA computes Spearman $\rho$ between cosine-distance representation RDMs and Euclidean-distance action RDMs, with 10{,}000 bootstrap CIs (\S\ref{sec:rsa}).
MLP probes (input $\to$ 256 $\to$ 7; Adam, lr${}=10^{-3}$, 50 epochs, early stopping) report mean $R^2$ (\S\ref{sec:probes}).
This yields $N = 63{,}228$ observations (after excluding terminal frames without next-action labels; $63{,}728$ total); RSA uses task-level blocking ($N_\text{eff} = 10$) and probes use demo-level train/test splits.
We report standard Cohen's $d$ for unpaired token-level comparisons and paired $d_z = \bar{\Delta} / s_\Delta$ for within-subject effects (Appendix~\ref{sec:paired_effect_sizes}).
All patterns reproduce with a linear probe (Appendix~\ref{sec:linear_probe}).

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Framework Validation: Token-Level Specialization}
\label{sec:token_specialization}

Cross-instruction probes (\S\ref{sec:cross_instruction_probes}) validate the framework (Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}).
As predicted by the causal mask, image\_mean instruction classification stays at chance ($10\%$) across all 32 layers in all conditions; linear and MLP probes corroborate this (Appendix~\ref{sec:instruction_invariance_appendix}).
Trained readout achieves $100\%$ 10-way classification from L1 through L31 (LOOCV-confirmed; Appendix~\ref{sec:loocv}); untrained achieves $100\%$ at L1 but decays to $82\%$ by L31, confirming the readout as the primary multimodal integration site (QA probe $86.3\%$ vs.\ DINOv2 $82.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:qa_probe},~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification at selected layers (full profile in Appendix~\ref{sec:dense_routing}). Image\_mean remains at chance ($10\%$) in all conditions; trained readout reaches $100\%$ at L1 and holds; untrained readout decays from L1 to L31.}
\label{tab:dense_routing_main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Layer & Trained readout & Untrained readout & Image\_mean \\
\midrule
L0 & 10.0\% & 10.0\% & 10.0\% \\
L1 & 100.0\% & 100.0\% & 10.0\% \\
L4 & 100.0\% & 99.1\% & 10.0\% \\
L8 & 100.0\% & 98.2\% & 10.0\% \\
L16 & 100.0\% & 92.3\% & 10.0\% \\
L31 & 100.0\% & 82.0\% & 10.0\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{VLA Fine-Tuning Redistributes Instruction Attention}
\label{sec:learned_routing}

\paragraph{Three-level attention gradient at L1.}
We compare L1 readout attention across three conditions (Table~\ref{tab:routing_gradient}; Figure~\ref{fig:attention_profile}): untrained networks allocate 0/32 instruction-dominant heads ($11.7\%$ text); Llama-2 base 9/32 ($36.5\%$); VLA fine-tuning 24/32 ($57.7\%$), a redistribution beyond inherited priors.
Per-token normalization ($38.2\times$ text-to-image ratio; Appendix~\ref{sec:attention_decomp}) corroborates this redistribution.

\paragraph{Redistribution across depth.}
The 32-layer profile (Figure~\ref{fig:attention_profile}) shows qualitatively distinct patterns.
Llama-2 base concentrates instruction attention at mid-depth (L9--L16: 15--22 heads).
VLA fine-tuning shifts to an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1: 21--24 heads, L31: 18), with intermediate layers markedly lower (0--11 at L2--L30).
Head counts reflect single-sample extraction; threshold sensitivity analysis (30\%--70\%) confirms ordering robustness (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Task-specific sharpening.}
A fourth condition (OpenVLA-7B fine-tuned on LIBERO) shows stable L1 entry concentration (22/32 heads) but complete loss of L31 exit re-engagement (0 vs.\ 18 heads) and further mid-depth compression (L9--L16: 1--7 vs.\ 6--11), an $N=2$ pattern consistent with exit-layer attention serving multi-task flexibility.
Probe accuracy is preserved (L1: $99.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:finetuned_probe}).

\paragraph{Cross-architecture generalization.}
To test whether these patterns extend beyond a single architecture, we analyze TraceVLA~\cite{zheng2024tracevla}, a 4B VLA chaining Phi-3-V~\cite{abdin2024phi3} with CLIP, on 5 LIBERO-Goal tasks (matching its training set; pre-VLA baseline uses all 10).
Pre-VLA Phi-3-Instruct starts from the opposite extreme: 28/32 instruction-dominant heads at L1 ($77.5\%$ text attention) distributed across all layers (L3--L28: 10--32 heads), reflecting instruction-tuning.
VLA training suppresses mid-layer attention (L3--L28: 0--12 heads) while preserving entry-exit concentration (L1: 32/32, $73.5\%$; L29--L31: 17--18), arriving at a qualitatively similar entry-exit pattern to OpenVLA despite differing backbones, vision encoders, and action representations (Figure~\ref{fig:cross_arch_attention}).
Token-level specialization replicates (image\_mean at chance; readout $100\%$ from L2; Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_probe}).

\paragraph{Consistent reorganization from opposite starting points.}
The two architectures start from dramatically different untrained profiles: OpenVLA peaks at L0 ($R^2 = 0.629$) then collapses to ${\approx}0$ at mid-layers, while Phi-3-V peaks at L6 ($0.578$) with gradual decline.
Yet training drives both to similar mid-layer peaks (OpenVLA L9: $0.637$; TraceVLA L11: $0.634$; $\Delta = 0.003$; overlapping CIs; Table~\ref{tab:2x2_r2}); a TOST equivalence test confirms statistical equivalence at $\varepsilon = 0.05$ ($p < 0.0001$; 90\% CI $[-0.002, 0.009]$; Appendix~\ref{sec:equivalence_testing}).
Peak $R^2$ values are comparable before and after training ($91$--$99\%$ of trained), but peak locations shift deeper (L0${\to}$L9; L6${\to}$L11) and deep-layer variance drops substantially (OpenVLA std $0.277 \to 0.040$; Phi-3-V $0.068 \to 0.011$).
This consistency across maximally different starting points---one architecture building from near-random representations, the other from instruction-tuned features---is consistent with VLA training discovering a common functional organization constrained by the causal-decoder design, though observed in only two architectures.
L1 probe accuracy \emph{decreases} in TraceVLA ($98.1\% \to 83.6\%$) despite more instruction-dominant heads ($28 \to 32$), replicating the information-computation dissociation in OpenVLA: increased attention to instructions does not imply increased decodable information.

\paragraph{Probe robustness corroborates the pattern.}
Cross-instruction probes corroborate the three-condition gradient (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:probe_conditions_appendix}).
Training effects concentrate at the readout ($R^2$ $+0.069$, attention $+46$pp) while image\_mean changes minimally ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_effect_summary},~\ref{sec:action_r2_threecond}).

\paragraph{Open-loop functional sensitivity.}
We ablate attention to instruction tokens at the readout position.
Ablating all 24 instruction-dominant heads at L1 increases action $R^2$ by $+0.030$ (baseline $0.524$)\footnote{$R^2$ values differ from Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview} because the ablation pipeline reconstructs representations from modified attention outputs, yielding a different baseline ($0.524$) than the standard extraction pipeline ($0.630$); all comparisons are within-experiment.}.
We compare $R^2$ against 30 random subsets of 24 heads (Appendix~\ref{sec:random_subset_ablation}): random ablation yields a larger mean increase of $+0.071 \pm 0.013$ ($z = -3.11$, percentile $= 0\%$).
In the original extraction pipeline (\S\ref{sec:repr_extraction}), L1 ablation increases $R^2$ by $+0.059$ vs.\ no-ablation baseline, decaying at deeper layers (L8: $+0.018$; L13: $\Delta \approx 0$); instruction attention redistribution is an entry-layer phenomenon, consistent across all 10 tasks (Table~\ref{tab:per_task_r2}).

\paragraph{Action trajectory validation.}
Ablating 24 instruction-dominant heads alters $72.3\%$ of action tokens (token-level Cohen's $d = 0.65$, paired $d_z = 1.17$; trajectory-level $d = 2.943$, Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}); control ablation (8 heads) yields token-level $d = 0.41$ ($29.3\%$), concentrated on the gripper dimension (shift $0.291$ vs.\ $< 0.034$; Tables~\ref{tab:attention_decomp},~\ref{tab:per_dim_mae}).

\paragraph{Dose-response gradient.}
Ablating 4--32 instruction-dominant heads yields monotonic divergence ($d = 0.482 \to 0.639$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), with steep rise through 12 heads and plateau beyond 20 (Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).

\paragraph{Counterfactual instruction analysis.}
Replacing the instruction with four perturbation types across all 10 LIBERO-Goal tasks produces divergence largely ordered by token novelty ($\rho = 0.8$): empty ($d = 0.978$), wrong task ($0.924$), shuffled ($0.763$), paraphrased ($0.707$; Table~\ref{tab:counterfactual}); effect sizes are consistent across tasks (CV: $0.17$--$0.30$).
The one ordering violation---shuffled ($0.763$) exceeding paraphrased ($0.707$) despite identical token sets---reveals that positional sensitivity can dominate token-level novelty.
The effect replicates across LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks): ordering is identical (empty $d = 0.942$ $>$ wrong $0.851$ $>$ shuffled $0.833$ $>$ paraphrased $0.806$; all $d > 0.8$; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:counterfactual_spatial}).
Spatial instructions differ only in prepositions; wrong-task $d = 0.851$ demonstrates that instruction-dominant heads route fine-grained spatial information, not merely task identity.
Across both suites (20 tasks), the counterfactual gradient is consistent ($d = 0.71$--$0.98$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Instruction attention at L1. Mode: immediate/gradual/incidental $=$ acquisition speed; +stable/+forget $=$ retention pattern. Accuracy gap (99.6\% vs.\ 100.0\% in Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}): task-level vs.\ LOOCV splits.}
\label{tab:routing_gradient}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Condition & Heads & Text attn & L1 acc & L31 acc & Mode \\
\midrule
VLA Trained & 24/32 & 57.7\% & 99.6\% & 100.0\% & immed.+stable \\
Llama-2 base & 9/32 & 36.5\% & 80.2\% & 100.0\% & gradual+stable \\
Untrained & 0/32 & 11.7\% & 100.0\% & 82.0\% & incid.+forget \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[b]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_32layer_attention.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[b]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\caption{(a) Instruction-dominant attention heads across 32 layers for three conditions (untrained, Llama-2 base, VLA-trained). VLA training concentrates instruction attention at L1 (24/32 heads) with L31 re-engagement. (b) Dose-response ablation of L1 instruction-dominant heads: progressive removal yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.48 \to 0.64$), with steep rise through 12 heads and plateau beyond 20. See Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory} for per-condition details.}
\label{fig:attention_profile}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Task-dependent attention modulation.}
The redistribution effect is task-dependent: instruction-discriminative tasks show $2.5\times$ larger $|\Delta R^2|$ under L1 ablation than visually-sufficient tasks ($p = 0.033$; Table~\ref{tab:task_complexity}), rising to $6.5\times$ ($p = 0.008$) under a stricter criterion.
No single task drives the effect (Appendix~\ref{sec:task_complexity}).

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Non-Monotonic Refinement of Vision Encoder Output}
\label{sec:nonmonotonic}

\paragraph{Vision encoder provides geometric foundation.}
Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview} reports RSA $\rho$ and probe $R^2$ between representations and actions.
DINOv2 \texttt{cls\_token} achieves the highest RSA ($\rho = 0.692$), exceeding the VLA peak ($0.628$ at L12) and CLIP ($0.557$); this ordering is metric-dependent (CKA reverses it; Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}).

\paragraph{Middle-layer peak (metric-dependent).}
Under RSA and action-probe decodability, visual representations show a middle-layer peak: trained image\_mean $\rho$ is stable through L12 ($0.628$), then declines to $0.422$ at L32 (Figure~\ref{fig:layer_curves}); per-seed peak $R^2$ averages $0.655 \pm 0.002$ (optimal layer L8--L10; Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_sensitivity}).
The readout achieves $R^2 = 0.678$ (L13), exceeding the projector by $\Delta = 0.048$ (Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview}).
Under linear CKA, this pattern disappears (monotonic increase; Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}); the non-monotonic trajectory characterizes rank-order geometry specifically, not global covariance structure.

\paragraph{Training primarily enhances readout decodability.}
Best-vs-best RSA gain is non-significant on LIBERO-Goal ($\Delta\rho = 0.017$, $3\%$, $N{=}10$ tasks; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:training_effects}), though LIBERO-Spatial yields a significant geometric gain ($\Delta = +0.023$, $p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}).
Post-hoc power analysis confirms that $N_{\text{eff}} = 10$ reliably detects $R^2$ effects of the observed magnitude (power $= 1.0$ for $\Delta = 0.069$) but is underpowered for the smaller RSA effect ($\Delta = 0.017$, power $= 0.36$; Appendix~\ref{sec:statistical_power}); non-significant differences should not be interpreted as evidence of absence.
Functional improvements are larger: readout $R^2$ $\Delta = +0.069$ ($p < 0.0001$, 95\% CI $[0.047, 0.090]$), image\_mean $\Delta = +0.015$ ($p = 0.006$), surviving Benjamini--Hochberg FDR correction ($\alpha = 0.05$, 33-layer family per metric) at 29--30/33 layers.
A global Bonferroni correction across all 63 tests preserves all core findings (Appendix~\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{RSA $\rho$ and probe $R^2$ alignment with actions. DINOv2 achieves highest RSA ($0.692$); ordering is metric-dependent (Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}). VLA rows: peak values. \textbf{Bold}: best; \underline{underline}: second.}
\label{tab:rsa_overview}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Source & RSA $\rho$ & $R^2$ & RSA Peak & $R^2$ Peak \\
\midrule
DINOv2 cls\_token & \textbf{0.692} & 0.634 & --- & --- \\
VLA Trained readout & \underline{0.643} & \textbf{0.678} & L12 & L13 \\
Fused DinoSigLIP & 0.634 & --- & --- & --- \\
VLA Trained image\_mean & 0.628 & \underline{0.655} & L12 & L8--L10$^\dagger$ \\
DinoSigLIP+Projector & 0.615 & 0.630 & --- & --- \\
VLA Untrained image\_mean & 0.612 & 0.640 & L0 & L0 \\
VLA Untrained readout & 0.626 & 0.609 & L1 & L32 \\
CLIP ViT-L/14$^\ddagger$ & 0.557 & 0.587 & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize $^\dagger$Per-seed peak $R^2$ across 5 seeds ($0.655 \pm 0.002$; Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_sensitivity}).} \\
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize $^\ddagger$RSA uses \texttt{patch\_mean}; $R^2$ uses \texttt{cls\_token} (each encoder's best aggregation).}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer RSA ($\rho$). Trained image\_mean: inverted-U peaking at L12; untrained decays monotonically. Dashed lines: standalone encoders.}
\label{fig:layer_curves}
\end{figure}

